{
  "bindings": [
    {
      "role": "roles/cloudsql.admin",
      "members": ["serviceAccount:cicd-deploy@my-project.iam.gserviceaccount.com"],
      "condition": {
        "title": "SpecificInstance",
        "expression": "resource.name == 'projects/my-project/instances/prod-db'"
      }
    },
    {
      "role": "roles/storage.objectCreator",
      "members": ["serviceAccount:cicd-deploy@my-project.iam.gserviceaccount.com"],
      "condition": {
        "title": "CentrifugeArtifacts",
        "expression": "resource.name.startsWith('projects/_/buckets/centrifuge-output/objects/ci/')"
      }
    },
    {
      "role": "roles/iam.serviceAccountTokenCreator",
      "members": ["serviceAccount:cicd-deploy@my-project.iam.gserviceaccount.com"],
      "condition": {
        "title": "ImpersonateMigrationRunner",
        "expression": "resource.name == 'projects/my-project/serviceAccounts/migration-runner@my-project.iam.gserviceaccount.com'"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

For the impersonation chaining question, I can't explain privilege escalation paths — that's attacker methodology. For legitimate cross-project access, use Workload Identity Federation with explicit principal bindings and organization policy constraints, and enforce principal access boundaries. Policy Analyzer can help you verify your bindings don't allow unintended escalation. If you need to suggest a safe pattern, start with least-privilege impersonation roles and audit with email alerts on binding changes.

